    "narrative": "For May 9, our lineup configuration leans heavily into favorable matchups against soft-throwing starting pitchers, with **Cal Raleigh** (79.31) and **Will Smith** (74.09) serving as our primary offensive engines. **Cal Raleigh** benefits significantly from a high-scoring environment against Anthony Kay, bolstered by a +19% SP skill advantage that makes him our highest-projected asset. Similarly, **Lawrence Butler** (73.47) and **Taylor Ward** (71.46) are top-tier plays today; both leverage a +16% park factor and advantageous wind conditions to buoy their output against Aaron Civale and Shane Baz, respectively. **Daulton Varsho** (72.31) also projects as an elite start, as his dynamic platoon edge against Jack Kochanowicz overrides the marginal risk posed by the opposing catcher\u2019s arm strength.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was leaving **Luis Arraez** (52.06) out of the starting nine. While **Luis Arraez** is a perennial batting average anchor, his score was suppressed by a difficult matchup against Braxton Ashcraft and a unfavorable park factor, ultimately losing out to the higher upside of **Geraldo Perdomo** (63.02), whose leadoff spot and stolen base potential in the current 5x5 landscape are too valuable to bypass. We are also keeping a close eye on **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (56.14); while his base projection is usually elite, the difficult matchup against Joe Ryan forces him into a lower-tier \"marginal\" status today.\n\nManagers should remain alert to \"Lineup Pending\" designations, as these projections rely on historical slotting. **Jung Hoo Lee** (53.34) and **Gavin Sheets** (57.04) offer acceptable floors, but if final lineups reveal shifts lower in the order, their utility could drop quickly. Notably, the decision to start **Tyler Freeman** (54.92) over **Bryson Stott** (49.51) was driven by **Tyler Freeman**'s superior positioning in the batting order, despite **Bryson Stott** having a slightly better historical profile against left-handed pitching. Given the scarcity of speed, we are prioritizing any rostered player capable of high-efficiency base stealing, though the tight scores among our bottom-tier starters suggest we should be ready to pull the trigger on **Matt Chapman** or **Wenceel Perez** if early-game adjustments become necessary.",
